_:rd5783ea9f8c3dce62070c919_n4efe36b43a6e44f9b64d34056015dea4b5 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#value> "Software" .
_:rd5783ea9f8c3dce62070c919_n4efe36b43a6e44f9b64d34056015dea4b6 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#predicate> <https://concepts.datalad.org/s/things/v2/description_html> .
_:rd5783ea9f8c3dce62070c919_n4efe36b43a6e44f9b64d34056015dea4b6 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://concepts.datalad.org/s/things/v2/AttributeSpecification> .
_:rd5783ea9f8c3dce62070c919_n4efe36b43a6e44f9b64d34056015dea4b6 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#value> "<p> <a href=\"https://github.com/con/mechababs\">mechababs</a> runs <a href=\"https://bids-apps.neuroimaging.io\">BIDS Apps</a> over many studies on an HPC cluster, with <a href=\"https://github.com/PennLINC/babs\">BABS</a> running the jobs. It works on a BIDS study, adding derivatives to it, and records how each one was made inside the study itself: the exact mechababs and BABS versions pinned in a lock, and every orchestration step as a <a href=\"#datalad_\">DataLad</a> run record. A superstudy groups many studies so one campaign sweeps them all, which is how mechababs feeds <a href=\"https://github.com/OpenNeuroStudies/OpenNeuroStudies\">OpenNeuroStudies</a>. </p>" .
_:rd5783ea9f8c3dce62070c919_n4efe36b43a6e44f9b64d34056015dea4b7 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#predicate> <http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/homepage> .
